Pack the parameters into a **struct** and pass a pointer to it:
```c
typedef struct { int rows, cols; } thrarg_t;

void *loopthread(void *arg) {
    thrarg_t *thrarg = (thrarg_t *) arg;    // cast back
    for (j = 0; j < thrarg->rows; j++)
        for (k = 0; k < thrarg->cols; k++)
            printf("loop iteration %d\t%d\n", j, k);
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t tid;
    thrarg_t thrarg;
    thrarg.rows = 10; thrarg.cols = 20;
    pthread_create(&tid, NULL, loopthread, &thrarg);
    pthread_join(tid, NULL);
}
```

**Why `pthread_join` is necessary:** `thrarg` is a **local variable of `main`**. The thread only gets a **pointer** to it.
- If `main` returned without joining, `thrarg` would cease to exist while the thread may still be reading it.
- **Returning from `main` ends the whole process**, killing the thread before it has finished.

The join keeps `main` (and `thrarg`) alive until the thread is done.

```c
thrarg_t thrarg;
thrarg.rows = 10; thrarg.cols = 20;
pthread_create(&tid1, NULL, loopthread, &thrarg);
thrarg.rows = 20; thrarg.cols = 30;          // overwrites the SAME struct
pthread_create(&tid2, NULL, loopthread, &thrarg);
pthread_join(tid1, NULL); pthread_join(tid2, NULL);
```

**Problem:** both threads get a **pointer to the same struct**. If the main thread **overwrites `thrarg` before thread 1 has read it**, thread 1 runs with 20 × 30 instead of 10 × 20. Whether this happens **depends on timing**, so it is a **race condition**: it may work in testing and fail later.

**Fix:** give **each thread its own argument struct**, and keep it alive until the thread is done:
```c
thrarg_t args[2] = { {10, 20}, {20, 30} };
pthread_create(&tid1, NULL, loopthread, &args[0]);
pthread_create(&tid2, NULL, loopthread, &args[1]);
```
(or `malloc` one struct per thread and let the thread `free` it).

Typical in labs: when creating threads in a loop, **don't pass `&i`** (the loop variable changes). Pass `&ids[i]` with a separate array.

The threads of a process **share one address space** and **can be interrupted by the scheduler at any point**, so **race conditions can occur**.

**Example:** two threads compute a scalar product into the shared variable `sum`:
```c
// Thread 1                          // Thread 2
for (i = 0; i < n/2; i++)            for (i = n/2; i < n; i++)
    sum += a[i] * b[i];                  sum += a[i] * b[i];
```
`sum += …` is load–add–store, and the threads' steps can interleave so that **updates are lost**, giving a wrong result.
(The slide has Thread 2 start at `n/2+1`. That would also skip element n/2.)

**Slow fix:** lock a mutex around every `sum +=`. This is correct, but the threads then take turns, and with n locks it's slower than sequential.

**Good fix:** accumulate into a **local (private) variable**, and lock only **once per thread**:
```c
double local = 0;
for (i = lo; i < hi; i++) local += a[i] * b[i];
pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
sum += local;
pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
```
**Pthreads coordination mechanisms:** mutex variables and condition variables.

```c
pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
while (!AnyCondition())
    pthread_cond_wait(&c, &m);
/* condition holds AND we hold m: do the work */
pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
```

**The mutex m protects both the evaluation of the condition and the `pthread_cond_wait()` call.** So **the condition can't change between the test and the start of the wait**. Without that, a signal could arrive in the gap and be lost.

**Requirement:** **all threads must lock m before changing any variable in the condition.**

**Why `while`, not `if`:**
- After being woken up, the thread must **compete for m** again. Another thread may get m first and **make the condition false again** (e.g. two consumers are woken up but there is only one item).
- `pthread_cond_broadcast` wakes **all** waiters, but maybe only one can proceed.
- POSIX also allows **spurious wake-ups** (waking without a signal).

The `while` loop simply **re-checks** and goes back to waiting if needed.

```c
void SignalCond() {                  // each worker, when it finishes
    pthread_mutex_lock(&data.m);
    data.numThreads--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&data.m);
    pthread_cond_signal(&data.c);    // signal AFTER unlock
}
void WaitForCond() {                 // control thread
    pthread_mutex_lock(&data.m);
    while (data.numThreads > 0)
        pthread_cond_wait(&data.c, &data.m);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&data.m);
    pthread_cond_destroy(&data.c);
}
```
**Problematic order:**
1. Worker **T1** runs SignalCond(), decrements numThreads to **0**, unlocks m, and is **interrupted before signalling**.
2. The control thread **T2** runs WaitForCond(), sees numThreads == 0, **doesn't wait**, and **destroys data.c**.
3. T1 continues and calls `pthread_cond_signal` on a **destroyed condition variable**: **error**.

**Fix:** move `pthread_cond_signal()` **before** `pthread_mutex_unlock()` in SignalCond(). T2 can't get past the lock (and destroy c) until T1 has both decremented *and* signalled.

```c
int pthread_cond_timedwait(pthread_cond_t *c, pthread_mutex_t *m,
                           struct timespec *t);
```
Like `pthread_cond_wait`, but **with a time limit**: the blocking on c is **cancelled as soon as the time t has passed**. It then returns **ETIMEDOUT** (after re-locking m).

```c
struct timespec {
    time_t tv_sec;    // seconds
    long   tv_nsec;   // nanoseconds
};
```
**Important:** t is an **absolute time** (a point in time), **not an interval**. Get the current time with `time()` (`<time.h>`) or `gettimeofday()` (`<sys/time.h>`), and add the timeout:
```c
struct timespec t;
t.tv_sec = time(NULL) + 5;     // wait at most 5 s from now
t.tv_nsec = 0;
while (!ready && rc != ETIMEDOUT)
    rc = pthread_cond_timedwait(&c, &m, &t);
```
*Use:* avoid waiting forever, e.g. give up or report an error if a worker doesn't answer in time.

```c
#define N 10
int buf[N], count = 0, in = 0, out = 0;
pthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_cond_t not_full  = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;
pthread_cond_t not_empty = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;

void put(int x) {                       // producer
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    while (count == N)                  // full: wait
        pthread_cond_wait(&not_full, &m);
    buf[in] = x; in = (in + 1) % N; count++;
    pthread_cond_signal(&not_empty);    // wake a consumer
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
}

int get(void) {                         // consumer
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    while (count == 0)                  // empty: wait
        pthread_cond_wait(&not_empty, &m);
    int x = buf[out]; out = (out + 1) % N; count--;
    pthread_cond_signal(&not_full);     // wake a producer
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
    return x;
}
```
**Uses everything from the lecture:** the mutex protects the buffer and the conditions, **`while` loops** re-check after waking, the **signal happens while holding the mutex** (no lost signals), and there are **two condition variables**, so producers and consumers wake each other and not their own kind.

**Mutexes and condition variables can be used to build more complex synchronization mechanisms.**

**Reader/writer (R/W) lock:** access control for a data structure where
- **any number of reads can take place simultaneously**,
- but **only one write at a time**, and no reads during a write.

✔ **More parallelism** than a plain mutex when the data is **read often and written rarely** (e.g. a lookup table or cache): readers don't block each other.

**Simple implementation:**
```c
typedef struct {
    pthread_mutex_t m;     // protects the counters
    pthread_cond_t  c;     // wait here for access
    int num_r, num_w;      // current read / write permissions
} rw_lock_t;

int rw_lock_init(rw_lock_t *rwl) {
    rwl->num_r = rwl->num_w = 0;
    pthread_mutex_init(&rwl->m, NULL);
    pthread_cond_init(&rwl->c, NULL);
}
```
Pthreads also has a built-in version: `pthread_rwlock_t` with `pthread_rwlock_rdlock` / `pthread_rwlock_wrlock`.

**Read request:** granted only if **no thread has write permission**. Any number of readers is fine.
```c
int rw_lock_rlock(rw_lock_t *rwl) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&rwl->m);
    while (rwl->num_w > 0)                       // a writer is active
        pthread_cond_wait(&rwl->c, &rwl->m);
    rwl->num_r++;                                // one more reader
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&rwl->m);
    return 0;
}
```

**Write request:** granted only if **no other thread has read or write permission**.
```c
int rw_lock_wlock(rw_lock_t *rwl) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&rwl->m);
    while (rwl->num_w > 0 || rwl->num_r > 0)     // anyone inside
        pthread_cond_wait(&rwl->c, &rwl->m);
    rwl->num_w = 1;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&rwl->m);
    return 0;
}
```
**Note:** the mutex `m` is held only **while checking and updating the counters**, not during the actual read/write of the data. That is what lets several readers work at the same time.

**Releasing a read permission:**
```c
int rw_lock_runlock(rw_lock_t *rwl) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&rwl->m);
    rwl->num_r--;
    if (rwl->num_r == 0)                 // last reader leaves
        pthread_cond_signal(&rwl->c);    // wake a waiting writer
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&rwl->m);
}
```
When the **last reader** leaves, a waiting **writer** is woken. **No reader can be waiting** at that point, because readers only wait while a *writer* holds the lock, and a reader held it.

**Releasing a write permission:** wake **all** waiting threads with **broadcast**, since several readers may now enter together:
```c
rwl->num_w = 0;
pthread_cond_broadcast(&rwl->c);
```

**Writer starvation:** this implementation **favours readers**. If reader A holds the lock and writer B waits, **new readers are still let in** (they only check `num_w`). As long as readers keep arriving, `num_r` never reaches 0, so **B may never get to write**.
*Fix:* also count **waiting writers**, and make new readers wait if a writer is waiting.

**Stack size** (if `_POSIX_THREAD_ATTR_STACKSIZE` is defined, or `sysconf(_SC_THREAD_ATTR_STACKSIZE)` succeeds):
```c
pthread_attr_getstacksize(&attr, &stacksize);
pthread_attr_setstacksize(&attr, stacksize);   // minimum size in bytes
```
The default minimum depends on the implementation (`PTHREAD_STACK_MIN`). **Portability can be lost**, since not all libraries support changing it.
*Why change it?* A thread with large local arrays or deep recursion may overflow the default stack.

**Stack start address** (`_POSIX_THREAD_ATTR_STACKADDR`): `pthread_attr_setstackaddr(...)`. **Changing it is dangerous!**

**Example from the slides:**
```c
pthread_attr_init(&attr);
pthread_attr_setdetachstate(&attr, PTHREAD_CREATE_DETACHED);
#ifdef _POSIX_THREAD_ATTR_STACKSIZE
    pthread_attr_getstacksize(&attr, &stacksize);
    pthread_attr_setstacksize(&attr, stacksize * 2);   // double the stack
#endif
pthread_create(&tid, &attr, f, NULL);
pthread_exit(NULL);
```
It creates a **detached** thread with **twice the default stack**. `main` ends with **`pthread_exit`** instead of `return`, so the process keeps running until the detached thread finishes (`return` from main would end the whole process).

**Problem:** you want a variable that **lives as long as the thread** but **belongs to one thread only**. The options:
- **global variable:** ✘ all threads can access it
- **pass it as a parameter to every function:** ✘ clumsy, every call needs it
- **thread-specific data:** ✔ **process-global keys** to which **each thread associates its own value**

**API:**
- `pthread_key_create(&key, destructor)`: create a key. The **destructor** is called with the thread's value **when the thread terminates**. **Each key may only be created once**, so use `pthread_once`.
- `pthread_setspecific(key, value)`: store a **pointer** (not the data itself) for **the calling thread only**.
- `pthread_getspecific(key)`: returns **the value the calling thread stored**.
- `pthread_key_delete(key)`: remove the key. **The stored data is lost.**

```c
static pthread_key_t key;
static pthread_once_t once = PTHREAD_ONCE_INIT;
static void make_key(void) { pthread_key_create(&key, free); }

char *my_buffer(void) {            // one buffer per thread
    pthread_once(&once, make_key);
    char *b = pthread_getspecific(key);
    if (!b) { b = malloc(256); pthread_setspecific(key, b); }
    return b;
}
```
